# Test: do markets work?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "A test across the whole block, with new cases"
next: {"title":"Why do we buy what we buy?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/71-why-do-we-buy-what-we-buy.cast"}

## Firewood in a cold snap
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: The woodcutters would hire you as their economist.
    good: Mostly warm. One draught to seal.
    poor: A chilly start, but the curves will thaw.
    none: Cold snap. Try the curves once more.
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Cords of wood (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand:
      offset: 0
    supply: {}
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
    tax:
      amount: 0
      label: S + tax
    price_ceiling:
      level: 48
      label: cap
    regions:
      - deadweight_loss
    box: full

    card title "Test: firewood in a cold snap"

Do markets work? Time to test the whole block on cases we haven't met, starting with firewood in Harbourtown.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S

B: The wood sells where the curves cross. Then an early, bitter winte (@highlight demand_curve supply_curve@)r arrives. Which curve moves, and which way?
    draw equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar

    ask "Where does demand for firewood go? Move it, then press Done." on demand_curve predict true store t
        right: Yes: demand moves right. In the cold, people want more wood at every price.
        wrong: Not quite: demand moves right. In the cold, people want more wood at every price.

    keep demand_curve label_D

Demand moves right: at every price, people want more wood. The price climbs, and the woodcutters bring more to market.
    animate demand.offset 20 duration 3

B: Then the complaints reach the harbour council, and it caps firewood at the old, pre-winter price.
    draw ceiling_line label_ceiling

    quiz "The council caps firewood at the old, pre-winter price. What follows?"
        right: A shortage: at the old price buyers want more wood than the woodcutters will bring.
        wrong: Compare what buyers want at the old price with what sellers bring: a shortage.
        + A shortage: more wanted than offered
        * A surplus of unsold wood
        * Nothing changes

At the old price, buyers want far more wood than the woodcutters bring. That gap is the shortage.
    draw shortage_guide_qd label_Qd shortage_guide_qs label_Qs shortage_arrow label_shortage

B: So the wood goes by queue, by luck, or by who knows the woodcutter. Not to whoever is coldest.
    highlight shortage_arrow label_shortage

Drop the cap and tax each cord instead.
    erase ceiling_line label_ceiling shortage_guide_qd label_Qd shortage_guide_qs label_Qs shortage_arrow label_shortage demand_curve_ghost label_D_ghost

    animate price_ceiling.level 96 duration 0.1

    draw tax_supply_curve label_S_tax dwl_region

The wedge between what buyers pay and sellers keep kills trades worth making: that lost gain is the triangle.
    animate tax.amount 14 duration 2.5

    draw label_DWL

B: Now double the tax. How much bigger does the triangle get?
    animate {"box":{"x":20,"y":20,"w":680,"h":650}} duration 1.2

    scale times x 730 y 420 width 240 label "times bigger" min 1 max 8 value 4

    ask "Click how many times bigger the triangle gets." on times store g
        right: Yes, four times: twice as tall and twice as wide.
        wrong: You said {g}. Twice as tall and twice as wide makes four.

About four times. The triangle grows in height and in width at once,  (@highlight dwl_region@)so the waste rises with the square of the tax.
    animate tax.amount 28 duration 3

    erase times

    animate box full duration 1.2

B: And who pays it?
    draw tax_guide_lines price_buyers_point label_Pb price_sellers_point label_Ps

B: Pause and turn the demand curve steeper: the less buyers can cut back, the bigger their share.
    explore

So the price tool passes: a cap brings a queue, a tax brings a triangle, and the side that can't dodge pays.
    highlight dwl_region tax_supply_curve

## Two cafés on the quay
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Both cafés would like you on their side.
    good: A decent hand at the game.
    poor: The quay is a tricky place to do business.
    none: Even the coffee cart is ahead of you there.
use: payoff_matrix
with:
    row_player: Café A
    col_player: Café B
    row_strategies:
      - Close at six
      - Stay late
    col_strategies:
      - Close at six
      - Stay late
    payoffs:
      - [[300, 300], [150, 400]]
      - [[400, 150], [200, 200]]
    highlight_equilibrium: true
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 200
      w: 880
      h: 420

    card title "Test: two cafés on the quay"

B: Many sellers passed. Now just two: the cafés on Harbourtown's quay, each deciding whether to stay open late.
    draw grid row_player_label col_player_label row_label_0 row_label_1 col_label_0 col_label_1

B: Illustrative daily profits, in dollars. If both close at six, three hundred each.
    draw cell_0_0

B: If one stays late, it takes the other's evening crowd: four hundred against one fifty.
    draw cell_1_0 cell_0_1

B: If both stay late, they split the evening and both pay extra staff: two hundred each.
    draw cell_1_1

Easy, then. They both close at six; anyone can see that's better.
    highlight cell_0_0 effect box

    ask "Where do the two cafés end up? Tap the cell." choose ["cell_0_0","cell_0_1","cell_1_0","cell_1_1"] answer cell_1_1 voice b
        right: Both stay late: whatever the other does, staying late pays each café more.
        wrong: Check each café's best reply to each move of the other: staying late every time, so both stay late.

B: Each café's best reply is to stay late, whatever the other does. So both end on t (@highlight cell_1_1 cell_0_0@)wo hundred, with three hundred on the table.
    draw nash_1_1

So rivals are stuck in the worse cell for good?
    point at.ref cell_0_0 gesture circle

    quiz "The owners will be neighbours for twenty years and see each other's lights every night. What could keep both closing at six, with no law?" voice b
        right: The threat of staying late next week: in a repeated game, tomorrow's punishment can outweigh tonight's gain.
        wrong: Think what a cheat loses in the weeks that follow: the threat of staying late next week.
        + The threat of staying late next week if the other cheats
        * Nothing: tonight's logic still wins
        * Only a signed contract

B: Not if they meet again every night. A quiet deal to close early can hold, though it's also a deal against the customers.
    highlight cell_0_0

    animate {"box":{"x":180,"y":330,"w":640,"h":275}} duration 1.2

Then say one owner buys the other café. What do the customers lose?
    cards own y 270 ends ["customers keep most","customers keep least"]
        * Many cafés compete
        * One owner, one price
        * One owner, a price each

    ask "Rank the three markets by how much the customers keep." on own store k stage own
        right: Many cafés first, one price next, a price for each customer last.
        wrong: You got {k}. Ask who takes the gap between what each customer would pay and the price: many cafés first, one price next, a price for each customer last.

B: One price means fewer cups at a higher price. A price for every customer takes the whole gain: as efficient as competition, and buyers keep nothing.
    highlight own

    erase own

    animate {"box":{"x":60,"y":200,"w":880,"h":420}} duration 1.2

Unless anyone can wheel a coffee cart onto the quay tomorrow.
    icon cart size 80 x 900 y 560 or ["hot beverage"] icon_look picture of coffee
    label cart_label "open entry?" below attach_to cart

B: Then the threat of entry holds the price down, but only while entry is cheap and quick to undo.
    highlight cart

    erase cart cart_label

So with few sellers, the trap that hurts them helps their customers: longer hours. The real risk is the quiet deal to escape it.
    highlight cell_0_0

## Who fixes what
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: The harbour council should hire you.
    good: A sound eye for where markets crack.
    poor: The checklist is worth a second look.
    none: Even the pilots would want a recount.
sources:
    - id: ireland_levy
      title: 'Plastic bag levy: per-capita use fell from an estimated 328 to 21 bags'
      authors: Irish government; Convery, McDonnell and Ferreira (Environmental and Resource Economics)
      year: 2007
      finding: After the €0.15 levy of March 2002, use fell from about 328 bags per person a year to about 21; retail bag use fell by roughly 90%.
    - id: taylor2019
      title: 'Bag leakage: The effect of disposable carryout bag regulations on unregulated bags'
      authors: Rebecca L. C. Taylor
      year: 2019
      finding: California's local bag bans cut plastic carryout bags by about 40 million pounds a year, but trash-bag purchases rose by about 12 million pounds, offsetting roughly 30%.

    icon bag size 150 x 300 y 540 or ["bag"] icon_look picture of "shopping bag" hidden true
    text levy "€0.15 a bag" cites ["ireland_levy"] x 560 y 555 font_size 44 hidden true
    text levy_year "Ireland, 2002" #8f887c x 560 y 500 font_size 26 hidden true
    scale bags cites ["ireland_levy"] x 150 y 330 width 700 label "bags per person a year" min 0 max 350 value 21 markers [{"value":328,"label":"before"}] hidden true
    scratch leak center cites ["taylor2019"] work ["California towns' bag bans:","about 30% came back","as bin bags"] font_size 30 hidden true
    grid check gap 30 columns 2 fit.h 320 fit.w 660 fit.x 170 fit.y 330 hidden true
    box n1 "Side effects" icon factory in check hidden true
    box n2 "Can't shut out" icon lighthouse in check hidden true
    box n3 "Hidden knowledge" icon detective in check hidden true
    box n4 "Few sellers" icon crown in check hidden true

    card title "Test: who fixes what"

One real case first. In 2002 Ireland put a fifteen-cent charge on every plastic shopping bag.
    draw bag levy levy_year

B: Before it, the estimate was about 328 bags per person a year. Where did it land?
    draw bags

    ask "Click where you think use landed, in bags per person a year." on bags store g voice b
        right: About 21: you said {g}, close to the truth.
        wrong: You said {g}; the drop was far steeper, to about 21.

To about 21, almost overnight. A small price on a cost nobody paid, the litter, did what years of appeals hadn't.
    highlight bags

    erase bag levy levy_year bags

B: But fixes leak. When California towns banned carryout bags, people bought more bin bags, and about thirty percent of the plastic saved came back.
    draw leak

So even a good fix needs checking. Here's the block's chec (@draw n1 n2 n3 n4 parallel true@)klist of the ways a market goes wrong.
    erase leak

B: Side effects nobody pays for, goods nobody can be shut out of, knowledge one side hides, and too few sellers. Sort these new cases.
    blocking false

    highlight n1 duration 1.5

    highlight n2 duration 1.5

    highlight n3 duration 1.5

    highlight n4 duration 1.5

    erase n1 n2 n3 n4

    cards sortc check each
        Side effects: Club noise
        Can't shut out: Flood wall, Open-sea fishing
        Hidden knowledge: Used phone, Insuring the ill
        Few sellers: The only bridge

    ask "Which failure does each case show? Put each card in its box." on sortc store f stage own
        right: All six right: the noise is a side effect, the wall and the open sea shut nobody out, the phone and the insurance hide knowledge, and the bridge has one seller.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. The noise is a side effect, the wall and the sea shut nobody out, the phone and the insurance hide knowledge, the bridge has one seller.

    erase sortc

Each box is a reason the price misses something. None of them says the council will do better.
    draw n1 n2 n3 n4 parallel true

B: Try one. Harbourtown's licensed pilots want a rule that every boat over five metres must hire one of them.
    text govfail "and the cure can fail too" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 285 font_size 40

    quiz "Harbourtown's licensed pilots ask the council to require a pilot on every boat over five metres. Who is most likely to win?" voice b
        right: The pilots: each gains a lot, each boat owner loses a little, so only the pilots find it worth organising.
        wrong: Compare the stake per pilot with the stake per boat owner: the pilots win.
        + The pilots: a big gain each, the cost spread thin
        * The boat owners: there are more of them
        * Whoever has the better argument

The pilots gain a lot each; every boat owner loses a little. That's government failure from incentives, beside the plainer kind: a council that can't know the right number.
    highlight govfail

B: Last one, and this time there's no right answer. The council auctions its ten best berths.
    highlight n1 n2 n3 n4

    quiz "The council auctions its ten best berths to the highest bidders. A good way to hand them out?" judge false voice b
        right: Both sides have a point: the auction is efficient in money terms, and whether that is fair is a question of values the figure cannot settle.
        * Yes: they go to whoever values them most
        * No: the richest boats win
        * Efficient, but fairness is a separate question

How sure are we? The tools themselves are close to consensus. Many verdicts are contested, case by case.
    text sure1 "the tools: consensus" #2f6b8f x 500 y 235 font_size 44
    text sure2 "many verdicts: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 180 font_size 44

So do markets work? Often, and for a reason. They fail in ways we can name, and any cure has to pass the same test as the market.
    highlight n1 n2 n3 n4 govfail

